Goth. ''thaursus'', dry, withered; akin to Goth. ''thairsan'', be dry, = L. ''torrere'' (orig. *''torsere''), parch with heat (cf. ''terra'' (*''tersa''), dry ground, the earth), = Gr. τέρσεσθαι, become dry (τερσαίνειν, dry up, wipe up), = Skt. √ ''tarsh'', thirst; cf. Ir. ''tart'', thirst, drought, etc. From the L. source are ult. E. ''torrent'', ''torrid'', ''terra'', ''terrene''<sup>1</sup>, ''terrestrial'', ''inter''<sup>1</sup>, etc.] 1. A feeling of dryness in the mouth and throat; the uncomfortable sensations arising from the want of fluid nutriment; the uneasiness or suffering occasioned by want of drink; vehement desire for drink. {{smaller|The sensations of thirst are chiefly referred to the thorax and fauces, but the condition is really one affecting the entire body. The excessive pains of thirst compared with those of hunger are due to the fact that the deprivation of liquids is a condition with which all the tissues sympathize. Every solid and every fluid of the body contains water, and hence abstraction or diminution of the watery constituents is followed by a general depression of the whole system. Thirst is a common symptom of febrile and other diseases. Death from thirst, as of persons in a desert, appears to be invariably preceded by acute mania.}}

{{smaller block|Than he commanded him to Presoun, and alle his Tresoure aboute him; and so he dyed for Hungre and ''Threst.''
{{right|''Mandeville'', Travels, p. 230.}}}}

{{smaller block|Raymounde tho lepte vp hys coursere vppon,
To the fantain and wel of ''thrust'' gan to go.
{{right|''Rom. of Partenay'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 765.}}}}

{{smaller block|Among sensations of Organic Life, I may cite ''Thirst'' as remarkable for the urgency of its pressure upon the will.
{{right|''A. Bain'', Emotions and Will, p. 318.}}}}

2. Figuratively, an ardent desire for anything; a craving.

{{smaller block|Over all the countrie she did raunge}}

