'''thirst''' (thėrst), ''v.'' [Early mod. E. or dial. also ''thrust, thrist;'' < ME. ''thirsten, thursten'', transposed ''thristen'', < AS. ''thyrstan'' = OS. ''thurstian'' = D. ''dorsten'' = MLG. ''dorsten'' = OHG. ''dursten'', MHG. G. ''dursten, dürsten'' = Icel. ''thyrsta'' = Sw. ''törsta'' = Dan. ''törste;'' from the noun; cf. Goth. ''thaursjan'', impers., thirst: see ''thirst'', ''n.'' Cf. ''athirst.''] '''I.''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To experience uncomfortable sensations for want of drink; have desire to drink; be dry.

{{smaller block|If thine enemy hunger, feed him; if he ''thirst'', give him drink. {{right|Rom. xii. 20.}}}}

'''2.''' To have a vehement desire; crave.

{{smaller block|My soul ''thirsteth'' for God. {{right|Ps. xlii. 2.}}}}

{{smaller block|Although the beauties, riches, honours, sciences, virtues, and perfections of all men living were in the present possession of one, yet somewhat beyond and above all this there would still be sought and earnestly ''thirsted'' for.
{{right|''Hooker'', Eccles. Polity, i. 11.}}}}

{{smaller block|He ''thirsted'' for all liberal knowledge.
{{right|''Milton'', Hist. Eng., v.}}}}

'''II.''' ''trans.'' To have a thirst for, literally or figuratively; desire ardently; crave: now usually followed by an infinitive as the object.

{{smaller block|The eternal God must be prayed to, . . . who also grant them once earnestly to ''thirst'' his true doctrine, contained in the sweet and pure fountains of his scriptures.
{{right|''Tyndale'', Ans. to Sir T. More, etc. (Parker Soc., 1850), p. 283.}}}}

{{smaller block|That unhappy king, my master, whom
I so much ''thirst'' to see. {{right|''Shak.'', W. T., iv. 4. 524.}}}}

{{smaller block|He seeks his Keeper's Flesh, and ''thirsts'' his Blood.}}

